## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design time-bounded data structures and integrate ordering with expirations.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window with time-based expirations
- Duplicates handled via unique id
- Order statistics tree for kth largest
- Online maintenance of a running sum

## Code Example
```javascript
// Implementation sketch
class SlidingWindowDurations {
  constructor(W) { this.W = W; this.queue = []; this.ost = new OrderStatisticTree(); this.nextId = 0; this.sum = 0; }
  insert(ts, dur) { const id = this.nextId++; this.queue.push({ts, dur, id}); this.ost.insert({dur, id}); this.sum += dur; }
  advance(now) { while (this.queue.length && this.queue[0].ts <= now - this.W) { const e = this.queue.shift(); this.ost.erase({dur: e.dur, id: e.id}); this.sum -= e.dur; } }
  sumInWindow() { return this.sum; }
  kthLargest(k) { const n = this.ost.size(); if (k < 1 || k > n) return null; const idx = n - k; return this.ost.find_by_order(idx).dur; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for variable window sizes per user?
- How do you handle memory pressure with very high event rates?
